<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Robust Multimodal Semantic Segmentation with Balanced Modality Contributions</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Jiaqi Tan
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname"><sup id="id1" class="ltx_sup">†</sup>Xu Zheng
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Fangyu Li
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Yang Liu
</span><span id="id2" class="ltx_note ltx_note_frontmatter ltx_thanks_correspondence ltx_role_thanks"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">thanks: </span><sup id="id2.1" class="ltx_sup">∗</sup> Corresponding author. Email: yang.liu@bupt.edu.cn  <sup id="id2.2" class="ltx_sup">†</sup> Project Lead.</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Multimodal semantic segmentation enhances model robustness by exploiting cross-modal complementarities. However, existing methods often suffer from imbalanced modal dependencies, where overall performance degrades significantly once a dominant modality deteriorates in real-world scenarios. Thus, modality balance has become a critical challenge for practical multimodal segmentation. To address this issue, we propose EQUISeg, a multimodal segmentation framework that balances modality contributions through equal encoding of modalities. Built upon a four-stage Cross-modal Transformer Block (CMTB), EQUISeg enables efficient multimodal fusion and hierarchical selection. Furthermore, we design a Self-guided Module (SGM) that mitigates modality imbalance by introducing a mutual guidance mechanism, enabling each modality to adaptively adjust its contribution and enhance robustness under degraded conditions. Extensive experiments on multiple datasets demonstrate that EQUISeg achieves significant performance gains and effectively alleviates the adverse effects of modality imbalance in segmentation tasks.</p>
  
</div>
<div class="ltx_keywords">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_keywords">Index Terms: </h6>Modality Balance, Multimodal Semantic Segmentation, Model Robustness

</div><span id="id3" class="ltx_note ltx_role_address"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">address: </span><sup id="id3.1" class="ltx_sup">1</sup>Beijing University of Posts and Telecommunications
<br class="ltx_break"><sup id="id3.2" class="ltx_sup">2</sup>HKUST(GZ), <sup id="id3.3" class="ltx_sup">3</sup>INSAIT, Sofia University “St. Kliment Ohridski”</span></span></span>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">With the rise of modular sensors, incorporating more modalities into semantic segmentation has become a mainstream direction for improving the robustness of segmentation models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>, <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>. The core idea is to leverage multimodal complementarity, enabling models to remain robust under extreme conditions such as low-light environments at night or sensor failure by exploiting the advantages of modalities unaffected by such conditions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Existing multimodal semantic segmentation models can be roughly categorized into three types: ① RGB-dominant approaches  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>. For example, CMNext <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite> extracts complementary information from other modalities through its self-query hub design to enhance RGB representations, while CMX <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite> effectively achieves RGB–modality fusion via FRM and FFM. However, the robustness of these methods heavily relies on the RGB modality. Once RGB becomes fragile under extreme conditions such as low-light or distortion, overall performance drops significantly. ② Adaptive modality selection approaches <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>, <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>, <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>. A representative work is MAGIC <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>, which dynamically selects the dominant modality by calculating the cosine similarity of modality features. Although theoretically sound, this approach often fails in practice due to quality differences across modalities. For instance, high-quality modalities like RGB and Depth tend to retain higher similarity scores even under extreme conditions compared to lower-quality modalities such as Event, causing the adaptive selection mechanism to malfunction. ③ Equal-treatment approaches <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>, <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>. A typical example is StitchFusion <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>, which encodes all modalities equally to obtain modality-specific information, and employs Modality Adapters at different density levels to achieve modality fusion. While these methods show great potential, existing models usually conduct fusion only at the feature level after self-attention encoding and through simple networks such as Feed-Forward Networks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite> rather than enabling information interaction during the feature extraction stage, which greatly limits fusion effectiveness.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To address these limitations, we propose EQUISeg, a novel multimodal segmentation framework that extends SegFormer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite> by integrating the strengths of three mainstream paradigms to achieve balanced representation and robust cross-modal fusion. EQUISeg consists of two core modules: the Cross-modal Transformer Block (CMTB) and the Self-Guided Module (SGM). The encoder is built from four hierarchical CMTBs, each adopting a primary–auxiliary scheme where one modality is encoded as primary and others as auxiliaries. This design emphasizes cross-modal complementarity and avoids over-reliance on a single modality, with self-attention capturing intrinsic features and cross-attention injecting informative auxiliary cues directly during representation learning. To further mitigate modality imbalance, SGM enforces equal contribution by randomized teacher–student guidance after prototype abstraction <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>, <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>, fundamentally reducing fixed modality dependence and enhancing robustness under diverse and degraded sensing conditions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Extensive experiments on the DeLiVER <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>, and MUSES <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite> datasets under both the mIoU benchmark and robustness evaluation criteria <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite> demonstrate that our framework achieves improvements of +1.56% and +1.85% compare with SoTA, respectively, outperforming previous methods.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Method</h2>

<figure id="S2.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.24505v1/mmss.png" id="S2.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/216;" width="476" height="216" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>Overview of the EQUISeg framework. Figure (a) shows the overall architecture, where multimodal data <math id="S2.F1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{r,d,e,l\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{r,d,e,l\}</annotation></semantics></math> are encoded through multi-layer attention and then fed into the segmentation head. The framework contains two core modules: the Cross-modal Transformer Block (CMTB) and the Self-Guided Module (SGM), with SGM only used during training. As shown in Figure (b), CMTB fuses auxiliary modalities via the Self-Query Hub (SQ Hub) and Parallel Pooling Mixer (PPX) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>, then integrates them with the primary modality through attention to capture complementary features. Figure (c) illustrates SGM, which assigns teacher–student roles and performs prototype-based self-guidance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>, <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>, encouraging equal modality contribution without altering the input or output.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To achieve robust and effective multimodal semantic segmentation, the proposed EQUISeg framework is constructed with two core modules: the Cross-modal Transformer Block (CMTB) and the Self-Guided Module (SGM). We will introduce the design of CMTB in Sec. <a href="#S2.SS1" title="2.1 Cross-modal Transformer Block ‣ 2 Method ‣ Robust Multimodal Semantic Segmentation with Balanced Modality Contributions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>, the mechanism of SGM in Sec. <a href="#S2.SS2" title="2.2 Self-Guided Module ‣ 2 Method ‣ Robust Multimodal Semantic Segmentation with Balanced Modality Contributions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>, and the overall architecture of EQUISeg in Sec. <a href="#S2.SS3" title="2.3 EQUISeg Architecture ‣ 2 Method ‣ Robust Multimodal Semantic Segmentation with Balanced Modality Contributions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.3</span></a>. The overall architecture is illustrated in Fig. <a href="#S2.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 2 Method ‣ Robust Multimodal Semantic Segmentation with Balanced Modality Contributions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, where the SGM is only used during training.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Cross-modal Transformer Block</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The detailed structure of the Cross-modal Transformer Block is shown in Fig. <a href="#S2.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 2 Method ‣ Robust Multimodal Semantic Segmentation with Balanced Modality Contributions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> (b). Obtaining pyramid features through multi-layer Transformer Blocks can effectively improve segmentation performance and has been widely adopted in various advanced multimodal frameworks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>, <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>. However, relying solely on self-attention to process multimodal information is insufficient. Therefore, the core of the Cross-modal Transformer Block is to introduce cross-attention <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite> to enhance modality fusion. Specifically, inspired by the dual-branch design of CMNeXt <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite> and the equal encoding strategy of StitchFusion <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>, we divide the input <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> modalities into a combination of one primary modality and <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N-1</annotation></semantics></math> auxiliary modalities. The <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N-1</annotation></semantics></math> auxiliary modalities are processed by the Self-Query Hub <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite> and PPX modules <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite> to select informative auxiliary features, which are then fused with the primary modality via cross-attention. Each modality is encoded independently to obtain rich and equally treated features. For example, when there are four input modalities <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{f_{1},f_{2},f_{3},f_{4}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{f_{1},f_{2},f_{3},f_{4}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, encoding modality <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> treats <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{f_{2},f_{3},f_{4}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{f_{2},f_{3},f_{4}\}</annotation></semantics></math> as auxiliary modalities. The whole process can be formulated as follows: <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{1}^{\prime}=\text{MHSA}(f_{1}),\;f_{\text{aux}}=\text{PPX}(\text{SQ Hub}(f_{2},f_{3},f_{4}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>MHSA</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>aux</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>PPX</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mtext>SQ Hub</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{1}^{\prime}=\text{MHSA}(f_{1}),\;f_{\text{aux}}=\text{PPX}(\text{SQ Hub}(f_{2},f_{3},f_{4}))</annotation></semantics></math>, where <span id="S2.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">MHSA</span> denotes the multi-head self-attention module in Segformer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">After obtaining the modality feature <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{1}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{1}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> and the fused auxiliary feature <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{aux}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{aux}</annotation></semantics></math>, residual cross-attention based feature fusion is performed, which can be formulated as follows:</p>
<table id="S2.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_gather ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle f_{c}=\text{MHCA}(f_{1},f_{2})=\text{Softmax}\left(\frac{Q_{1}K_{2}^{T}}{\sqrt{d_{k}}}\right)V_{2}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>MHCA</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>Softmax</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>T</mi></msubsup></mrow><msqrt><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msqrt></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle f_{c}=\text{MHCA}(f_{1},f_{2})=\text{Softmax}\left(\frac{Q_{1}K_{2}^{T}}{\sqrt{d_{k}}}\right)V_{2},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where the query is given by <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{1}=f_{1}^{\prime}W_{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{1}=f_{1}^{\prime}W_{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>, while the key and value are obtained as <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{2}=f_{aux}W_{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>K</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{2}=f_{aux}W_{K}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{2}=f_{aux}W_{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>V</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{2}=f_{aux}W_{V}</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively. The final output feature is obtained through a residual connection:</p>
<table id="S2.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_gather ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle f=f_{1}^{\prime}+f_{c}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle f=f_{1}^{\prime}+f_{c},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the final modality feature, <span id="S2.SS1.p2.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">MHCA</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite> represents the multi-head cross-attention mechanism, and <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{c}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the cross-modal feature obtained via cross-attention. In the EQUISeg architecture, multi-stage MTCBs are assigned to all modalities for balanced encoding, thereby effectively mitigating modality dependency.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Self-Guided Module</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The detailed structure of the Self-Guided Module is shown in Fig. <a href="#S2.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 2 Method ‣ Robust Multimodal Semantic Segmentation with Balanced Modality Contributions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> (c). At present, the mainstream approach to modality balancing is to design plug-and-play modules that take the features requiring balance as input, compute a loss function, and leverage this loss to guide balanced modality embedding <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>, <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>, <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite>. Meanwhile, cross-modal knowledge distillation has achieved remarkable results in modality transfer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>, <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite>. Therefore, we design a Self-Guided Module (SGM) that divides the modalities into teacher and student modalities. After prototypical representation of the features, the module performs modality information balancing through knowledge distillation. To avoid selection failure caused by quality differences among modalities, the grouping process is carried out randomly. Specifically, we firstly perform the following prototyping operation on the feature <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> with class <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S2.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=[p_{0},p_{1},\ldots,p_{C}],\quad p_{c}=\frac{\sum_{j}f^{j}\mathbbold{1}[l_{j}=c]}{\sum_{j}\mathbbold{1}[l_{j}=c]}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>C</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo>∑</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><mi>f</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>j</mi></msup><mn>𝟙</mn><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>𝕝</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>𝕛</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>𝕔</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>∑</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><mn>𝟙</mn><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>𝕝</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>𝕛</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>𝕔</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=[p_{0},p_{1},\ldots,p_{C}],\quad p_{c}=\frac{\sum_{j}f^{j}\mathbbold{1}[l_{j}=c]}{\sum_{j}\mathbbold{1}[l_{j}=c]},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the feature of pixel <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the prototype feature, and <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the ground truth label of pixel <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>. The indicator function <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbbold{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>𝟙</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbbold{1}</annotation></semantics></math> outputs 1 if the specified condition is satisfied and 0 otherwise. Taking DeLiVER as an example, at each training step the prototype features <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[p_{r},p_{d},p_{e},p_{l}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[p_{r},p_{d},p_{e},p_{l}]</annotation></semantics></math> are randomly divided into a teacher feature set <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[p_{t1},p_{t2}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[p_{t1},p_{t2}]</annotation></semantics></math> and a student feature set <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[p_{s1},p_{s2}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[p_{s1},p_{s2}]</annotation></semantics></math>. Modality self-guidance is then performed by minimizing the Kullback–Leibler divergence, defined as
<math id="S2.SS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{KL}}(f_{t},f_{s})=\sum_{c=1}^{C}f_{t}(c)\log\frac{f_{t}(c)}{f_{s}(c)}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>KL</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>C</mi></msubsup><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{KL}}(f_{t},f_{s})=\sum_{c=1}^{C}f_{t}(c)\log\frac{f_{t}(c)}{f_{s}(c)},</annotation></semantics></math>
where <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the teacher and student features, respectively. Based on this, the self-guidance loss is computed as</p>
<table id="S2.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_gather ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{s}=\sum_{i=1}^{4}\big(\mathcal{L}_{\text{KL}}(f_{t1}^{i},f_{s1}^{i})+\mathcal{L}_{\text{KL}}(f_{t2}^{i},f_{s2}^{i})\big)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>4</mn></munderover><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>KL</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>KL</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{s}=\sum_{i=1}^{4}\big(\mathcal{L}_{\text{KL}}(f_{t1}^{i},f_{s1}^{i})+\mathcal{L}_{\text{KL}}(f_{t2}^{i},f_{s2}^{i})\big),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th Transformer block. When the number of modalities is odd, one modality is randomly ignored in each round before performing the above grouping operation. This interleaved self-guidance strategy effectively prevents modalities from overfitting to high-quality ones, thereby better exploiting the complementary information contained in low-quality modalities.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.3 </span>EQUISeg Architecture</h3>

<div id="S2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in Fig. <a href="#S2.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 2 Method ‣ Robust Multimodal Semantic Segmentation with Balanced Modality Contributions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>(a), the M-Segformer adopts a multi-scale encoder–decoder architecture. The multi-scale encoder consists of a four-stage multi-branch encoder, where the number of branches corresponds to the number of modalities. Each branch specifically encodes one modality, while treating the others as auxiliary modalities that are fused through the Cross-modal Transformer Block (CMTB in Sec.<a href="#S2.SS1" title="2.1 Cross-modal Transformer Block ‣ 2 Method ‣ Robust Multimodal Semantic Segmentation with Balanced Modality Contributions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>). The four-stage structure follows prior advanced Transformer models to extract pyramid features <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>, <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite>, which are then fed into the segmentation head <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite> for semantic segmentation. Specifically, for the input multimodal data <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{r,d,e,l\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{r,d,e,l\}</annotation></semantics></math>, the operations of EQUISeg can be formulated as follows:</p>
<table id="S2.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{f_{r}^{i},f_{d}^{i},f_{e}^{i},f_{l}^{i},\dots\}_{i=1}^{4}=\text{CMTB}_{i=1,n=1}^{4,N}(\{r,d,e,l\dots\})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>l</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>4</mn></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mtext>CMTB</mtext><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{f_{r}^{i},f_{d}^{i},f_{e}^{i},f_{l}^{i},\dots\}_{i=1}^{4}=\text{CMTB}_{i=1,n=1}^{4,N}(\{r,d,e,l\dots\}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where, <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{r,d,e,l,\dots\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{r,d,e,l,\dots\}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the input raw modality data, <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> represents the features obtained from the <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th Transformer block, <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the total number of modalities, and <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> is the modality index. For example, in the DeLiVER dataset, <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=4</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{f_{r}^{i},f_{d}^{i},f_{e}^{i},f_{l}^{i},\dots\}_{i=1}^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>l</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>4</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{f_{r}^{i},f_{d}^{i},f_{e}^{i},f_{l}^{i},\dots\}_{i=1}^{4}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the final multi-stage modality feature outputs. Then, the features are fed into the Self-Guided Module (SGM in Sec.<a href="#S2.SS2" title="2.2 Self-Guided Module ‣ 2 Method ‣ Robust Multimodal Semantic Segmentation with Balanced Modality Contributions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>) for modality balancing, yielding the balanced loss <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{s}</annotation></semantics></math>. The formulation of <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> is as follows:</p>
<table id="S2.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{s}=\text{SGM}(\{f_{r}^{i},f_{d}^{i},f_{e}^{i},f_{l}^{i},\dots\}_{i=1}^{4})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>SGM</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>l</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>4</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{s}=\text{SGM}(\{f_{r}^{i},f_{d}^{i},f_{e}^{i},f_{l}^{i},\dots\}_{i=1}^{4}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.3" class="ltx_p">The calculation method is as shown in Eq. <a href="#S2.E4" title="In 2.2 Self-Guided Module ‣ 2 Method ‣ Robust Multimodal Semantic Segmentation with Balanced Modality Contributions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>. Finally, the features are fed into the segmentation head for segmentation <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y=\text{SegHead}(\{f_{r}^{i},f_{d}^{i},f_{e}^{i},f_{l}^{i},\dots\}_{i=1}^{4})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>SegHead</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>l</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>4</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y=\text{SegHead}(\{f_{r}^{i},f_{d}^{i},f_{e}^{i},f_{l}^{i},\dots\}_{i=1}^{4})</annotation></semantics></math> , and the cross-entropy loss is obtained as follows:</p>
<table id="S2.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_gather ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E7.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{\text{CE}}=-\sum^{C}_{c=1}g_{c}\text{log}(\text{Softmax}(y_{c}))," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>CE</mtext></msub><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.055em">−</mo><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>C</mi></munderover><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mtext>log</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>Softmax</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{\text{CE}}=-\sum^{C}_{c=1}g_{c}\text{log}(\text{Softmax}(y_{c})),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.4" class="ltx_p">where, <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of classes, and <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the ground truth. By combining Eq. <a href="#S2.E6" title="In 2.3 EQUISeg Architecture ‣ 2 Method ‣ Robust Multimodal Semantic Segmentation with Balanced Modality Contributions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> and <a href="#S2.E7" title="In 2.3 EQUISeg Architecture ‣ 2 Method ‣ Robust Multimodal Semantic Segmentation with Balanced Modality Contributions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>, the final loss can be obtained as follows:</p>
<table id="S2.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}=\mathcal{L}_{\text{CE}}+\lambda\mathcal{L}_{s}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>CE</mtext></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}=\mathcal{L}_{\text{CE}}+\lambda\mathcal{L}_{s},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.5" class="ltx_p">where, <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the loss during training. <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> is a hyperparameter that controls the balancing strength.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>EXPERIMENTS</h2>

<figure id="S3.tab1" class="ltx_table">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure ltx_flex_table">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<div id="S3.tab1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_figure_panel ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:345.0pt;height:55.1pt;vertical-align:-23.8pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(57.3pt,-9.2pt) scale(1.49764631562799,1.49764631562799) ;">
<table id="S3.tab1.1.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="S3.tab1.1.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.tab1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_tt" rowspan="2"><span id="S3.tab1.1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text"><span id="S3.tab1.1.1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text"></span> <span id="S3.tab1.1.1.1.1.1.2" class="ltx_text">
<span id="S3.tab1.1.1.1.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
